{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c001",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The defined term \"Defendants\" is made to cover \"these original and proposed additional defendants,\" so it is uncertain whether persons never actually joined to the Lawsuit are bound by or benefit from the agreement. The label is a defined-term boundary problem rather than mere transcription noise.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.10}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c002",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.12},
     {"issue": "This is essentially a recital describing the reinsurance arrangement, and a party could argue it narrows the operative claim-limitation in the third paragraph to claims under that described arrangement. The tension with the operative scope provision is possible but faint.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c003",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The provision purports to \"limit the scope\" of the claims but then says the permitted category \"includes, but is not limited to\" a list and \"may encompass ... among other things\" a further list of causes of action, so the outer reach of the retained claims is undefined. That general-catch-all-against-enumerated-specifics structure is exactly what a court must construe to decide whether a given claim survived the limitation.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.66},
     {"issue": "The open-ended retained-claims category cannot easily be squared with the immediately following paragraph excluding the \"Other Causes of Action\" (improper reserves, claims mishandling, closing of files), since a claim about reserves or claim payments could fall within both. Reconciling the two lists is a genuine whole-instrument question.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.36}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c004",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"Other Causes of Action\" is defined by an enumerated list with a parenthetical carve-back (\"other than concerning the exclusion or misapplication of IBNR\"), leaving it uncertain whether a reserve- or claims-handling theory that also touches IBNR is excluded or retained. The scope of the list against the IBNR proviso is the construable point.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.48},
     {"issue": "The exclusion overlaps with the retained IBNR/commission claims defined in the preceding \"limit the scope\" paragraph, which itself is expressly non-exhaustive, so the same claim may be both retained and disclaimed. The two paragraphs must be harmonised.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.34}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c005",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.13},
     {"issue": "The reservation of the right to sue on Other Causes of Action sits against the very next sentence barring any such separate lawsuit during the pendency of the Lawsuit, and against the Defendants' tolling promise, raising a question whether the reserved right is meaningfully preserved. The tension is manageable but nameable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c006",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"During the pendency of the Lawsuit\" is undefined and could mean until trial judgment or until all appeals are exhausted. The term controls when Lincoln General may file, so its boundary matters.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.35},
     {"issue": "The filing bar keyed to \"pendency\" does not line up with the Defendants' tolling promise, which runs until the Lawsuit is \"settled or otherwise finally resolved, including the completion of any appeals,\" so the two clocks may end at different moments. That mismatch could leave a gap in which suit is barred or limitations run.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"Agrees to enable ... U.S. Auto to limit the claims it might otherwise seek\" states no identifiable obligation or standard, and re-defines \"U.S. Auto\" although it was already defined in the opening paragraph. What Lincoln General must actually do to \"enable\" is unstated.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.32},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.11}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The \"Excess Funds Claim(s)\" are defined by reference to \"the maximum profit margin to which U.S. Auto contends Lincoln General is entitled,\" a party-dependent and unquantified benchmark, and the parenthetical definition is left unclosed. Whether a later-asserted claim is an Excess Funds Claim, and thus tolled and non-precluded, turns on that phrase.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.50},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.14},
     {"issue": "The stipulation that the Excess Funds Claims share no nucleus of operative facts with the Lawsuit is hard to reconcile with the scope paragraph, which retains commission and remittance claims under the same Quota Share Reinsurance Agreements. A court asked to apply preclusion would have to reconcile the two.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.14},
     {"issue": "The express waiver of res judicata and collateral estoppel conflicts in direction with the final paragraph, in which both sides \"reserve the right to contest any later-filed separate lawsuits\" and to assert \"any and all applicable defenses.\" Which sentence governs a preclusion defense is a real question.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.20},
     {"issue": "The tolling runs until final resolution \"including the completion of any appeals,\" while the following filing bar is keyed to \"the pendency of the Lawsuit,\" so the periods may not coincide. The duplicated \"the the\" is transcription noise, not the point.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
   ]}
]}

